00:00:54,871 --> 00:00:56,362
couple of functions that allow us to

25
00:00:56,378 --> 00:00:58,526
interact with that hashing function and with

26
00:00:58,558 --> 00:01:01,285
the underlying hash table array. Before we

27
00:01:01,285 --> 00:01:03,823
do that, however, let me clarify one thing.

28
00:01:04,642 --> 00:01:07,163
Why do we need to translate the key into

29
00:01:07,163 --> 00:01:10,006
such an index, into such a hash? Why don't

30
00:01:10,102 --> 00:01:11,998
we just use the key itself

31
00:01:12,592 --> 00:01:13,491
as an index?

32
00:01:14,214 --> 00:01:16,912
Now, there are multiple reasons for that.

33
00:01:17,587 --> 00:01:19,338
Depending on the programming language you're

34
00:01:19,434 --> 00:01:20,028
working with,

35
00:01:20,622 --> 00:01:24,461
you simply might not be able to use a string

36
00:01:24,654 --> 00:01:24,959
key

37
00:01:25,521 --> 00:01:28,570
as an index. If you just have something like

38
00:01:28,650 --> 00:01:31,745
a default array where the index values are

39
00:01:31,889 --> 00:01:35,128
numbers, you can't just use a string. So

40
00:01:35,144 --> 00:01:36,828
then you would need a function that

41
00:01:36,828 --> 00:01:39,730
translates your string into such an index.

42
00:01:40,291 --> 00:01:42,328
But there also is another reason.

43
00:01:43,162 --> 00:01:46,032
If you just take the key which is provided

44
00:01:46,064 --> 00:01:49,191
by the user and you use that as an index,

45
00:01:49,576 --> 00:01:51,131
let's assume that would be possible.

46
00:01:51,740 --> 00:01:53,937
If you would do that, there is the danger

47
00:01:54,242 --> 00:01:57,113
that you have a hash table

48
00:01:57,771 --> 00:02:01,188
where your index values, where your keys

49
00:02:01,765 --> 00:02:04,958
are not spread out evenly. Now, it really

50
00:02:04,990 --> 00:02:08,278
depends on the environment and the exact use

51
00:02:08,278 --> 00:02:11,134
case whether that matters, but it could lead

52
00:02:11,391 --> 00:02:15,016
to inefficient memory usage, storage, and

53
00:02:15,016 --> 00:02:17,470
therefore to performance disadvantages

54
00:02:18,128 --> 00:02:20,551
if your keys are not spread out evenly

55
00:02:21,112 --> 00:02:24,096
in memory, so to say. Now it's really a bit

56
00:02:24,144 --> 00:02:26,856
too advanced to really take a deep dive into

57
00:02:27,193 --> 00:02:29,182
all the details here, since that really

58
00:02:29,278 --> 00:02:30,706
leads us down the road to

59
00:02:31,284 --> 00:02:33,915
how computers work internally, how memory is

60
00:02:33,963 --> 00:02:36,867
utilized, and that again depends way too

61
00:02:36,883 --> 00:02:39,931
much also on the exact environment in which

62
00:02:40,027 --> 00:02:41,616
you're running, on your programming

63
00:02:41,648 --> 00:02:42,642
language, and so on.

64
00:02:43,412 --> 00:02:45,225
The important takeaway just is that there

65
00:02:45,322 --> 00:02:48,596
might be good reasons for having well-spread

66
00:02:48,612 --> 00:02:52,032
out index values and therefore we might not

67
00:02:52,224 --> 00:02:54,745
want to take the keys provided by the user.

68
00:02:55,275 --> 00:02:57,185
And in addition, there might be technical

69
00:02:57,282 --> 00:02:59,642
reasons that simply don't allow us to take

70
00:02:59,722 --> 00:03:01,199
strings as keys.

71
00:03:01,937 --> 00:03:04,506
In the end, you also must not forget that if

72
00:03:04,506 --> 00:03:05,052
you really

73
00:03:05,726 --> 00:03:08,054
dig down into how a computer works,

74
00:03:08,632 --> 00:03:10,912
it works just with zeros and ones.

75
00:03:11,586 --> 00:03:13,818
So ultimately, of course, it depends on the

76
00:03:13,914 --> 00:03:15,777
programming language you're working with

77
00:03:16,082 --> 00:03:19,424
which features you can use and which kind of

78
00:03:19,617 --> 00:03:21,625
data structures are built into the

